Studies have found that lifestyle interventions, including exercise, a healthy diet, an active social life, and managing other health issues, can help delay cognitive aging.

The US POINTER study examines whether lifestyle interventions can protect cognitive function in older adults.

The US POINTER randomized clinical trial shows that moderate lifestyle changes in diet, exercise, and socialization can improve cognition in those at risk for dementia.

**********Web Search Result**********

A recent study published in JAMA and presented at the Alzheimer's Association International Conference provides strong evidence that lifestyle changes can slow cognitive decline in older adults. The study followed over 2,100 participants aged 60–79 who were at higher risk for dementia, assigning them to either a structured or less-structured lifestyle program over two years. The structured group showed significantly greater cognitive improvement, equivalent to slowing decline by one to two years. This group received guidance on health goals, weekly brain-training, and a rebate for purchasing blueberries, while the other group received general advice. Participants with genetic risk factors like the APOE4 mutation and those with early Alzheimer's biomarkers also benefited, reinforcing the power of behavioral interventions. Key elements of the cognitive-preserving lifestyle included exercise, healthy eating, adequate sleep, social engagement, and managing heart health. Researchers plan to follow participants for another four years to evaluate long-term effects on dementia onset. Experts stress the importance of these findings for clinicians and individuals, noting that even partial lifestyle changes can benefit brain health. The study aims to influence public health strategies and encourage proactive cognitive care. ([time.com](https://time.com/7306434/daily-routine-cognitive-decline-study/?utm_source=openai))

Another study published in the Journal of Prevention of Alzheimer's Disease suggests that personalized interventions, such as brain games, can slow or reverse brain shrinkage in older individuals. Majid Fotuhi and his team conducted a study with 127 elderly participants showing mild cognitive impairment, administering a range of cognitive improvement strategies including meditation, dietary adjustments, stress reduction, and neurofeedback. Over three months, participants completed an intensive brain training program. Results showed significant cognitive improvements in most participants, with MRI scans revealing hippocampus growth in some cases, reversing the age-related brain shrinkage. Although the study lacked a placebo group, the findings support the idea of neuroplasticity in the aging brain. Fotuhi intends to further investigate the long-term effects and isolated impacts of neurofeedback. ([time.com](https://time.com/4218125/brain-games-neurofeedback-aging/?utm_source=openai))

Additionally, a systematic review published in BMC Geriatrics examined the effectiveness of home-based lifestyle interventions on cognition in older adults with mild cognitive impairment (MCI). The review included 20 randomized controlled trials focusing on interventions such as diet, physical activity, stress reduction, and cognitive training. Nineteen studies reported significant improvements in cognitive performance between the experimental and control groups post-intervention for at least one aspect of cognition. The findings suggest that home-based lifestyle interventions have the potential to improve cognition in elderly patients with MCI. However, the authors note that future trials with larger sample sizes and longer intervention durations are needed to confirm these findings. ([bmcgeriatr.biomedcentral.com](https://bmcgeriatr.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s12877-024-04798-5?utm_source=openai))

These studies collectively highlight the potential of lifestyle interventions in delaying cognitive decline and improving brain function in older adults, particularly those at risk for or experiencing mild cognitive impairment.  No source extracted due to invalid syntax

